WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:07.720
Lolade Nwanze and her team in The Guardian
newsroom check the top social media topics

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:09.750
every morning.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:15.030
On Twitter, an advisor to the Nigerian president
alleges that an opposition party is offering

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:18.240
food and money in exchange for votes.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:22.670
The news speads quickly - so the Guardian
team decides to investigate.

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:28.270
“We researched the image and found out it
was a lie.

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:31.289
The photograph dated as far back as 2017.

00:00:31.289 --> 00:00:37.010
It was a photograph from an outreach done
by a charity foundation in Lagos.

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:40.570
So it wasn’t what she said it was.”

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:46.120
Shortly after the Guardian publishes its findings,
the politician's Tweets are deleted.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:51.280
The team here say they see fake stories like
this every day.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.629
“If it is text, it is a lot easier.

00:00:54.629 --> 00:01:02.359
You just need to call up a few people, whoever
is making this claim.

00:01:02.359 --> 00:01:04.810
If it is photographs it is also somewhat easy.

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:08.810
The technology is quite simple to handle,
you can reverse search and so on.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:13.200
But when it is video, it becomes a little
bit more complicated.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.189
Once video gets on social media, usually on
Twitter, you have the meta data stripped off.

00:01:17.189 --> 00:01:20.020
And sometimes this is what you need actually
to track a video.

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:24.290
So video makes it a bit complicated, and audio
files as well.”

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:30.190
To check these kinds of stories, the Guardian
has teamed up with some 20 other media outlets.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:34.920
They've formed a collective called 'Cross
Check Nigeria' to support each other in the

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.310
research process.

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:41.310
A story only goes online when four of the
different media organisations have looked

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:42.380
at it.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:45.729
Dayo Aiyetan is one of the coordinators.

00:01:45.729 --> 00:01:50.490
Around 50 journalists are involved in the
project.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:59.760
“Everywhere in the world, even in the most
technologically advanced US, politicians would

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:03.250
always use any means to gain advantage.

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:06.520
If it is by weaponizing information – they
will do that.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:12.880
But the important thing is, when they know
once they try it, there will be mechanisms

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:17.740
– use of of technology – that can also
out them.

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:23.830
And perhaps that will reduce the incidents
of politicians weaponizing information for

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:26.720
their advantage or to do damage.”

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:32.390
Aiyetan says the number of fake stories has
increased in the run up to the elections.

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:37.700
Nigeria is the most populous country in Africa
and it's divided along ethnic lines - which

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:39.690
often leads to conflict.

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:45.030
According to Nigerian police, there have already
been cases of fase reports causing violence.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:51.420
So it is not only newspapers who are reacting
to increasing levels of fake news – even

00:02:51.420 --> 00:03:01.350
Facebook is acknowledging the issue: 10 tips
to spot false news– A tech giant advertising

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.710
in a traditional newspaper.

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:07.380
Lolade Nwanze thinks there has been a change
in the news business in recently.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:12.241
Rather than publishers competing against each
other to be the first to break a story, they

00:03:12.241 --> 00:03:16.010
are now working together to separate fact
from fiction.

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:21.960
In times of falling newspaper circulation,
she says, this could be an opportunity for

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:23.900
Nigerian media companies.

